<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albany, New York</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albany is the capital of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a>. It grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fur-trading post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-orange">Fort Orange</a>, built in 1624 near an earlier post of 1614, and the village of Beverwijck. After the English conquest of 1664 it was renamed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a> and Albany; it was chartered as a city in 1686 and became the state capital in 1797.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albany-new-york.jpg" alt="Albany, New York" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albany,_New_York.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Albany lies on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a>, about ten miles (16 km) south of the river's confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a>, and about 135 miles (217 km) north of New York City. It sits toward the north end of the navigable Hudson. The city is the economic and cultural core of New York State's Capital District, a metropolitan area that includes Schenectady, Saratoga Springs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> area was first inhabited by Algonquian-speaking Mohicans, who called it Pempotowwuthut-Muhhcanneuw, meaning the fireplace of the Mohican nation. The Iroquoian-speaking Mohawk, based to the west along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a>, called it skahnéhtati, through the pine woods, after the path they took there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the English captured New Netherland in 1664 the name Beverwijck was changed to Albany, in honour of Prince James, Duke of Albany, later King James VII and II. Duke of Albany was a Scottish title, given since 1398 generally to a younger son of the King of Scots. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> briefly regained the town in August 1673 and called it Willemstadt; the English took permanent possession in 1674 under the Treaty of Westminster. Governor Thomas Dongan chartered Albany as a municipality on 22 July 1686, as a strip of land one mile wide and sixteen miles long, with about 500 people.</p><p>Albany was a centre of transportation for much of its history. By 1815 it was the turnpike centre of the state, and with canals and railroads it became the hub for pioneers travelling to Buffalo and the Michigan Territory. It was the original eastern terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> and was home to some of the earliest railroads in the world. Democratic Party politics dominated the city from the 1920s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a>'s machine overcame the Republican one built in the 1890s by William Barnes Jr. Erastus Corning 2nd was mayor from 1942 until his death in 1983.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Albany is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and the seat of Albany County. Its population was 99,224 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is governed by a mayor and a Common Council under the Dongan Charter of 1686, revised by referendum in 1998, which gives it the oldest active city charter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>The Empire State Plaza, built between 1965 and 1978 under Governor Nelson A. Rockefeller and the architect Wallace Harrison, dominates the view of the city. Beside it stands the 19th-century New York State Capitol, which houses the State Legislature and the governor's office. The New York State Museum, the New York State Library and the New York State Archives are in the Cultural Education Center at the south end of the plaza. The First Church in Albany, established in 1642, is the second-oldest Reformed Church in America.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albany, New York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some 4,000 British immigrants, the 1820 Settlers, were placed in the <b>Albany</b> district of the Eastern Cape, and English replaced Dutch as the language of government and courts in the 1820s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mohawk traded beaver pelts with the Dutch at Fort Orange, founded in 1624 on the site of present-day <b>Albany</b>, for metal tools, cloth and firearms, and later traded with the English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander Hamilton, who served as George Washington's aide in the Revolutionary War, married Elizabeth Schuyler at the Schuyler Mansion in <b>Albany, New York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
